\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs46 \cf2 Installing Kernel and Kernel Extension Variants
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 The KDK includes several variants of the kernel binary and kernel extensions. These variants include additional assertions and error checking beyond what is present in the shipping version of the macOS kernel, and may be useful for debugging in various situations. Choose the variant that best suits your needs:\
\pard\tx20\tx180\pardeftab720\li180\fi-180\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\f2\i kernel
\f0\i0  (release) variant matches the shipping kernel for users. \
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\f2\i kernel.development
\f0\i0  (development) variant is safe for everyday use during development, and has minimal performance overhead. \
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\f2\i kernel.kasan
\f0\i0  (kasan) variant has more significant performance and memory overhead, and enables address sanitizer features that will, for example, panic the kernel upon memory safety violations.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When performing two-machine debugging, install these kernel variants on the target device.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf2 Note
\f0\i0 : Apple silicon doesn\'92t support installing the kernel and kernel extension variants from the KDK.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs30 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf2 Install the Variants on an Intel-based Mac
\fs30 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs28 \cf2 To install new kernel and kernel extension variants on the target device, perform the following steps:\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\b \cf2 Step 1: Install the KDK
\fs30 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0\fs28 \cf2 Install this KDK on the target device.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i \cf2 Step 2: Modify the Security Configuration of Your Mac\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 Modify the security configuration of your Mac by disabling SIP and enabling permissive security:\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reboot your Mac in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT201314"}}{\fldrslt Recovery Mode}}. \
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2.	}If your device {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT208862"}}{\fldrslt has the Apple T2 Security Chip}}, set {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://support.apple.com/en-us/HT208330"}}{\fldrslt the Secure Boot policy}}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 to \'93Reduced Security\'94. \
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3.	}Launch Terminal from the Utilities menu and run the following commands to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/disabling_and_enabling_system_integrity_protection"}}{\fldrslt disable System Integrity Protection (SIP)}} and Authenticated Root protection:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl1\cf3 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}
\f4 csrutil disable
\f0 \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}
\f4 csrutil authenticated-root disable
\f0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reboot your Mac.\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\b \cf2 Step 3: Identify the System Volume Disk Device Name\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 Run the 
\f4 \cf3 mount
\f0 \cf2  command in Terminal to identify the devices of your system volume snapshot. Look for the device mounted at \'93/\'94, which identifies the system volume disk. In the following example, this disk is 
\f4 \cf3 /dev/disk4s5s1
\f0 \cf2 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 /dev/disk4s5s1 on / (apfs, sealed, local, read-only, journaled)\
devfs on /dev (devfs, local, nobrowse)\
/dev/disk4s4 on /System/Volumes/VM (apfs, local, noexec, journaled, noatime, nobrowse)\
/dev/disk4s2 on /System/Volumes/Preboot (apfs, local, journaled, nobrowse)\
/dev/disk4s1 on /System/Volumes/Data (apfs, local, journaled, nobrowse)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf2 To get the actual name of the system volume disk, remove the final \'93s
\f2\i X
\f0\i0 \'94 from the device. In the preceding example, the name of the system volume disk is 
\f4 \cf3 /dev/disk4s5
\f0 \cf2 .\
\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\b \cf2 Step 4: Mount a Live Version of the System Volume\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 Run the 
