\f3\i\b \cf2 Step 6: Verify the Release Variant is Running\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 Verify you have restored the \'93release\'94 kernel variant by running the 
\f4 \cf4 sysctl
\f0 \cf2  command in Terminal to check the 
\f4 \cf4 kern.osbuildconfig
\f0 \cf2  property:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf4 sysctl kern.osbuildconfig\
kern.osbuildconfig: release
\f0 \cf2 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa180\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs46 \cf2 Configuring a Core Dump Server
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 You can use another Mac to capture core dumps from your system automatically over the network. This may be useful for kernel panics that occur early in the boot cycle, or when the kernel fails to write the core-dump file to disk after a panic. You can use a core dump server simultaneously with two-machine debugging. Use the following terminology to refer to the Macs associated with core dumps:\
- The 
\f2\i server
\f0\i0  device is the Mac that receives the core-dump files.
\fs46 \

\fs28 - The 
\f2\i target
\f0\i0  device is the Mac that generates the core-dump file.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs34 \cf2 Configure the Server Device
\fs30 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs28 \cf2 Configure the server device to receive the core dump files by performing the following steps:\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i \cf2 Step 1: Create the PanicDumps Directory
\fs30 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0\fs28 \cf2 Create a 
\f4 \cf4 /var/tmp/PanicDumps
\f0 \cf2  directory on the server, and configure it to be writable by any user. This directory is where the server writes the core-dump files. \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 mkdir \cf4 /var/tmp/PanicDumps\cf3 \uc0\u8232 chmod 1777 \cf4 /var/tmp/PanicDumps\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf2 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\b \cf2 Step 2: Load the kdumpd Launch Daemon\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 Run the 
\f4 \cf3 launchctl
\f0 \cf2  command in Terminal to load the \cf3 kdumpd\cf2  launch daemon. This service listens for incoming core dumps and writes them to 
\f4 \cf3 /var/tmp/PanicDumps
\f0 \cf2 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 sudo launchctl load -w /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.apple.kdumpd.plist\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs34 \cf2 \
Configure the Target Device
\fs30 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs28 \cf2 The same compatibility requirements apply to the target device as for two-machine debugging. You must also configure the target device as if you\'92re using it for two-machine debugging. (See: "Configure Apple silicon as a Target Device\'94 or \'93Configure an Intel-based Mac as a Target Device\'94 in the Performing Two-Machine Debugging section above)\
After performing those configuration steps, perform the following additional steps:\
\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i\b \cf2 Step 1: Set additional boot-args\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 Run the 
\f4 \cf3 nvram
\f0 \cf2  tool in Terminal to add the following arguments to your target device\'92s 
\f4 \cf4 boot-args
\f0 \cf2  key: \
\pard\tx540\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls17\ilvl3\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f4 \cf3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
debug=0xc44
\f0 \cf2 \'97Tells the kernel to create a core dump when a panic or NMI occurs.\
\ls17\ilvl3\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f4 \cf3 _panicd_ip=10.0.40.2
\f0 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\'97Set the value of this key to the IP address of the server device.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 sudo nvram boot-args=\'93debug=0xc44 kdp_match_name=en1 wdt=-1 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 _panicd_ip=10.0.40.2\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\i \cf2 Step 2: Reboot the Device\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0\b0 \cf2 After you reboot the target device, it sends core dumps to the server in the following situations:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls18\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}If the target device panics.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}If you {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://developer.apple.com/documentation/kernel/generating_a_non-maskable_interrupt"}}{\fldrslt trigger an NMI}} on the target device.\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Core dumps can be very large, so it may take some time for the core dump to transfer over the network. Additionally, ensure the server device has sufficient free disk space.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\fs34 \cf2 Finish Up\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs28 \cf2 When you no longer want to gather core dumps from the target device, return that device to normal operation (See: \'93Finish Up Your Debugging Session\'94 in the Performing Two-Machine Debugging section above)
\f4 \cf3 \

\f0 \cf2 When you no longer wish to capture core dumps on the server device, run the following to unload the 
\f4 \cf4 kdumpd
\f0 \cf2  launch daemon:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f4 \cf3 sudo launchctl unload -w /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.apple.kdumpd.plist\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa120\partightenfactor0

\f0 \cf2 \
}